"The diagnostic breaks down as follows:\n\n**`broken.c: In function 'main':`**\nContext line — gcc reports where it was in the source when parsing failed: inside the body of `main`.\n\n**`broken.c:3:30: error: expected ';' before 'return'`**\n- `broken.c:3:30` — file, line 3, column 30. Column 30 is just past the closing `)` of the `printf(...)` call, i.e., the exact spot where the statement should end.\n- `error:` — a hard syntax error (not a warning; `-Wall` is irrelevant here). gcc stops code generation and produces no output file.\n- `expected ';' before 'return'` — the C parser had just finished reading an expression statement (`printf(\"Hello, World!\\n\")`) and expected the mandatory terminating `;`. Instead, the next token it read was the keyword `return`. In C grammar, an expression statement must end with a semicolon, so the parser reports the mismatch by naming the token it actually found (`return`).\n\n**The source excerpt and carets:**\n```\n3 |     printf(\"Hello, World!\\n\")\n  |                              ^\n  |                              ;\n4 |     return 0;\n  |     ~~~~~~\n```\n- `^` at column 30 marks where gcc expected the `;` — immediately after the closing parenthesis.\n- `;` printed under the caret is gcc's suggestion of the missing token.\n- `~~~~~~` under `return` highlights the token that \"arrived too early\" — the parser tripped over it because the previous statement was never terminated.\n\n**In short:** line 3 is an expression statement missing its required semicolon. gcc detected this only when it reached the *next* token (`return` on line 4), which is why the error mentions `return` even though the actual defect is at the end of line 3. Because this is an error (not a warning), compilation exited with status 1 and no `broken` binary was created."